What is the difference between map() and flatMap()?”

map() transforms one element → one element, while flatMap() transforms one element → multiple elements and flattens the result.

CompletableFuture is used to perform asynchronous and non-blocking tasks efficiently. Introduced in Java 8,
it allows tasks to run in separate threads and provides powerful methods to manage, combine, and process results of 
asynchronous computations.

Part of the java.util.concurrent package and implements Future and CompletionStage.
Supports chaining multiple asynchronous tasks using methods like thenApply() and thenAccept().
Helps improve application performance by executing tasks concurrently without blocking the main thread.
Creating a CompletableFuture
CompletableFuture can be created using the supplyAsync() method, which runs a task asynchronously in a separate thread and 
returns the result later.

supplyAsync() is a static method of the CompletableFuture class.
It accepts a Supplier functional interface as an argument.
Supplier takes no input and returns a value.
